A `Channel` is used inside an arrow here: an arrow is lifted into a function of its own that the channel rules do not follow, so a channel is declared, sent on and received from only in a top-level function's own body; write the arrow as a module function, `const countTask = (out: Channel<boolean>): void => { out.send(true); out.send(false); }`, and pass `countTask` where the arrow was
